! jb_af1c F215 "LOAD FACTOR TEST", sub-block F215C "TEST CHANGED REGS",
! p.145 (octal 020231.40-020235.40).  Continues fp_arith_f215b.strap;
! F215D follows immediately at 020236.00, which is why this block has
! no exit branch of its own.
!
! THIS BLOCK CLOSES A GAP F215A AND F215B COULD NOT REACH.  Reference
! Manual p.98 says "FT bits 61-63 are set to zero"; F215A checks FT
! bits 0-59 and bit 60, F215B bit 60 alone, so neither can see it.  C's
! single check is exactly that: CM1111 fills the whole of word 14 with
! ones, an LFT follows, and CT0110(BU,3) over $FT+.61 demands the top
! three bits came back zero.
!
! IT IS STILL BLIND TO ONE FAULT, and the constant is why.  FPK2's own
! flag bits are ZERO, so an LFT that copied the operand's flags into FT
! 61-63 instead of zeroing them would write 000 here and pass.  What C
! catches is an LFT that leaves those bits ALONE.  Nothing in F215
! pairs an operand with non-zero flags against a check of FT 61-63 --
! F215A2 and F215B1 use FPK1, whose flags are 7, but neither looks
! above bit 60.  Measured, not assumed: see the plan doc's injection
! table.
!
! The comparison reads the accumulator's IBM 125-127 (a 3-bit field at
! offset 0), which the L(BU) above fills from FPK2's own low three
! bits -- zero.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)22007.0
FPK2    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777760

        SLC, (8)20231.40
FLF4    L(BU), FPK2
        CM1111(BU,64), $FT
        LFT(U), FPK2
        CT0110(BU,3), $FT+.61                ! were bits 61-63 of $FT set to zero
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                               ! 020236.00: F215D's first instruction
        END, FLF4
